"""`PhpCompat.cs` — PHP runtime semantics, seeded once instead of re-derived per file.

The PHP→.NET playbook (`playbooks/php_dotnet.py`) tells the conversion model to call
`PhpCompat.LooseEquals` "rather than re-deriving the rule per file". Nothing shipped it. Every
converted file that followed the instruction referenced a class that did not exist, so the
export could not compile — the playbook promised an API and no stage emitted it.

**Why this is a constant and not a generated file.** The same argument `scaffold/__init__.py`
makes about `.csproj` package versions, but sharper. PHP's `==` is a nine-row coercion table
that changed in PHP 8; its falsy set includes `"0"` but not `"0.0"`; `strpos` signals failure
with `false`, which is `0`-adjacent in every language that does not have PHP's type juggling.
A model asked to write that per file gets it *nearly* right, differently each time — and 200
subtly different implementations of truthiness is the class of bug nobody finds, because each
file reviews as correct in isolation and the disagreement only shows up as a wrong number in
production. Written from a constant, there is exactly one implementation, one review, and one
place to fix if it is wrong.

Same reason `scaffold/dotnet.py` exists and makes no model call: `stages.py` marks the
scaffold stage `llm=False`, and this file keeps that true.

**Scope.** This is a shim, not an emulator. Where PHP's behaviour cannot be reproduced
faithfully in C# (byte-vs-character offsets, `strtotime`'s natural-language parser, the
process-global timezone) the C# says so in its own doc comment rather than guessing quietly.
A reviewer reading `PhpCompat.cs` should come away knowing where the landmines are.

**Not wired up here.** This module emits and describes the artifact; it does not register
itself with `scaffold.build`. See `emit()` and `symbols()` for what an integrator must call.
"""
import logging
from typing import Any, Dict, List

from backend.services.modernization import playbooks

logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)

# The helper lives in `<root>.Compat` rather than the root namespace itself, so a converted
# file is never ambiguous about where `PhpCompat` came from. The `global using` alias inside
# the file is what keeps that free — see the C# header comment.
PHP_COMPAT_NAMESPACE_SUFFIX = "Compat"
PHP_COMPAT_FILENAME = "PhpCompat.cs"

CLASS_NAME = "PhpCompat"

# Substituted into the C# below. Not an f-string: the C# is almost entirely braces, and
# doubling every one of them to satisfy `str.format` would make the constant unreadable —
# which for a file whose whole value is that a human reviews it would be the wrong trade.
_NAMESPACE_TOKEN = "__PHPCOMPAT_NAMESPACE__"

# `analyzers/php.py` and `analyzers/generic.LANGUAGE_BY_EXT` both label PHP exactly "PHP".
_PHP_SOURCE_LANGUAGES = frozenset({"php"})

# A `.cs` file in an SDK-style VB.NET project is not a compile error — it is simply never
# compiled, which puts the run straight back to the defect this file exists to remove. Better
# to ship nothing and say so than to ship a file that is silently ignored.
_VB_TARGET_SPELLINGS = ("vbnet", "visualbasic", "vbdotnet")

# Matches `scaffold/dotnet.py`'s fallback project name, so a run with no usable project name
# still lands the helper somewhere a build can see it.
_FALLBACK_ROOT_NAMESPACE = "Converted"

_IDENTIFIER_CHARS = frozenset(
    "abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ0123456789_"
)


# ── The artifact ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# One self-contained static class. No package references, no `unsafe`, no reflection, no
# source generators — it has to compile in whatever project the converted code lands in,
# including one whose `.csproj` this platform did not write.

_PHP_COMPAT_CS = r"""// PhpCompat — PHP runtime semantics, written once and shared by every converted file.
//
// NOTHING IN THIS FILE WAS GENERATED BY A MODEL. It is emitted verbatim from a constant in
// the conversion platform, so every converted unit calls the same implementation of PHP's
// type juggling rather than each re-deriving it. If a rule here is wrong, it is wrong in
// exactly one place.
//
// READ THIS BEFORE TRUSTING IT:
//
//   * It implements **PHP 8** semantics. The string-to-number comparison rules changed in
//     PHP 8 (`0 == "foo"` was true in PHP 7 and is false in PHP 8). If the source codebase
//     ran on PHP 5 or 7 — which, being a codebase worth migrating, it very likely did — then
//     any place it relied on the old behaviour changes meaning here. That is a decision for
//     a reviewer, not something this file can paper over. See LooseEquals.
//   * Several methods document a behaviour they cannot reproduce faithfully (byte offsets,
//     `strtotime`, the process-global timezone). Those comments are the point of the file;
//     do not delete them when tidying.
//   * `ScriptTimeZone` is mutable process-global state. See its comment before using it in
//     a web application.
//
// The `global using` alias below is what lets converted code in any namespace of this
// project write `PhpCompat.LooseEquals(...)` with no `using` line of its own. Without it,
// every converted file would need `using __PHPCOMPAT_NAMESPACE__;` — and a file that forgets
// it fails to compile, which is the exact defect this file was added to remove.
//
// The alias covers **this project only**. If converted code lives in a second project, that
// project needs a project reference plus its own `using __PHPCOMPAT_NAMESPACE__;`.

#nullable enable

global using PhpCompat = __PHPCOMPAT_NAMESPACE__.PhpCompat;

using System;
using System.Collections;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Globalization;
using System.Text;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

namespace __PHPCOMPAT_NAMESPACE__;

/// <summary>
/// PHP runtime semantics that have no direct C# equivalent, in one place.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// Every method here exists because the obvious C# translation compiles and does something
/// different. Prefer a real C# idiom wherever the source's intent is clear — this class is
/// for the places where reproducing PHP's behaviour exactly is the requirement.
/// </remarks>
public static class PhpCompat
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Significant digits used when a float becomes a string, mirroring php.ini
    /// <c>precision</c> (default 14). Changing php.ini changed every number a legacy app
    /// echoed, so if the source deployment set a different value, set it here too.
    /// </summary>
    public static int PhpPrecision { get; set; } = 14;

    /// <summary>
    /// The timezone <c>date()</c>, <c>strtotime()</c> and <c>Now()</c> work in — the
    /// equivalent of PHP's <c>date_default_timezone_set()</c>.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// HAZARD. In PHP this was process-global and set once per request, which was survivable
    /// because a PHP process handled one request at a time. ASP.NET Core does not: this
    /// property is shared by every concurrent request in the application, so setting it
    /// per-request is a race, not a port. Set it once at startup to whatever the legacy
    /// server's php.ini said, and convert to a user's timezone at the display boundary
    /// instead. Defaults to UTC, which is almost certainly NOT what the legacy server used —
    /// leaving it at UTC silently shifts every date the application prints.
    /// </remarks>
    public static TimeZoneInfo ScriptTimeZone { get; set; } = TimeZoneInfo.Utc;

    // Arrays compare recursively and PHP fatals on a recursive structure. A StackOverflow
    // in .NET cannot be caught and takes the process with it, so comparison stops instead.
    private const int MaxComparisonDepth = 64;

    private static readonly Regex RelativeTimePattern = new Regex(
        @"^([+-]?\d+)\s*(second|sec|minute|min|hour|day|week|month|year)s?(\s+ago)?$",
        RegexOptions.CultureInvariant);

    // ── Truthiness ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// PHP's notion of truth — what <c>if ($x)</c> actually tests.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Falsy in PHP: <c>null</c>, <c>false</c>, <c>0</c>, <c>0.0</c>, <c>-0.0</c>, the empty
    /// string, the string <c>"0"</c>, and the empty array. Everything else is truthy.
    /// <para>
    /// The two that silently change behaviour when a developer writes <c>if (x != null)</c>
    /// instead of calling this:
    /// </para>
    /// <list type="bullet">
    /// <item><description>
    /// <c>"0"</c> is FALSY and <c>"0.0"</c> is TRUTHY. A form field posting the string "0" —
    /// an id, a quantity, a status code — takes the false branch in PHP and the true branch
    /// in any naive C# port.
    /// </description></item>
    /// <item><description>
    /// An empty array is FALSY. <c>if ($rows)</c> means "there are rows"; the C# port
    /// <c>if (rows != null)</c> means "the query ran", which is true for an empty result.
    /// </description></item>
    /// </list>
    /// <para>
    /// <c>NAN</c> is truthy in PHP, and so it is here. A <c>char</c> is treated as a
    /// one-character string, so <c>'0'</c> is falsy. Any other object is truthy, matching
    /// PHP's rule that an object instance always is.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    public static bool Truthy(object? value)
    {
        if (value is null) return false;
        if (value is bool flag) return flag;
        if (value is string text) return text.Length != 0 && !string.Equals(text, "0", StringComparison.Ordinal);
        if (value is char character) return character != '0';
        if (IsNumber(value)) return ToNumber(value) != 0d || double.IsNaN(ToNumber(value));
        if (value is IDictionary dictionary) return dictionary.Count > 0;
        if (value is ICollection collection) return collection.Count > 0;
        if (value is IEnumerable sequence) return AnyElement(sequence);
        return true;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// PHP's <c>empty()</c> — the exact negation of <see cref="Truthy"/>.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// One thing this CANNOT reproduce: in PHP, <c>empty($a['missing'])</c> is <c>true</c>
    /// and raises no warning, because <c>empty()</c> suppresses the undefined-index notice.
    /// In C#, reading a missing key from a dictionary throws <c>KeyNotFoundException</c>
    /// before this method is ever called. Convert <c>empty($a['k'])</c> to a lookup that
    /// tolerates a missing key (<c>TryGetValue</c>) and then this method — not to
    /// <c>Empty(a["k"])</c>, which turns a silent PHP default into a C# exception.
    /// </remarks>
    public static bool Empty(object? value)
    {
        return !Truthy(value);
    }

    // ── Comparison ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// PHP's <c>==</c> — loose comparison with type juggling. **PHP 8 semantics.**
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para>
    /// WHICH PHP. This implements the comparison table as it stands in PHP 8, including the
    /// "saner string to number comparisons" change. The difference a reviewer has to decide
    /// about:
    /// </para>
    /// <list type="bullet">
    /// <item><description><c>0 == "foo"</c> is <c>false</c> in PHP 8 and was <c>true</c> in PHP 7.</description></item>
    /// <item><description><c>0 == ""</c> is <c>false</c> in PHP 8 and was <c>true</c> in PHP 7.</description></item>
    /// <item><description><c>"abc" == 0</c> is <c>false</c> in PHP 8 and was <c>true</c> in PHP 7.</description></item>
    /// </list>
    /// <para>
    /// If the source codebase ran on PHP 5 or 7 — which is the usual reason a codebase is
    /// being migrated at all — then any guard written as <c>if ($input == 0)</c> against
    /// non-numeric input behaved the opposite way there. That is a real behavioural change,
    /// not a rounding error, and it is most dangerous in authorisation checks. Nothing in
    /// this file can decide it for you; the PHP 7 table is deliberately NOT implemented,
    /// because silently choosing the older semantics would be the same mistake in reverse.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>The PHP 8 rules, in the order applied here:</para>
    /// <list type="number">
    /// <item><description><c>null</c> versus a string: <c>null</c> becomes <c>""</c> and the two compare as strings. This row is why <c>null == ""</c> is true but <c>null == "0"</c> is false, even though both operands are falsy.</description></item>
    /// <item><description><c>null</c> or <c>bool</c> anywhere else: both sides become bool.</description></item>
    /// <item><description>Two numbers: numeric comparison.</description></item>
    /// <item><description>Number versus string: numeric if the string is numeric, otherwise the NUMBER becomes a string and the two compare as strings.</description></item>
    /// <item><description>Two strings: numeric if BOTH are numeric strings (so <c>"1" == "01"</c> is true), otherwise ordinal string comparison.</description></item>
    /// <item><description>Two arrays: equal counts, and every key present on both sides with loosely-equal values. Order does not matter.</description></item>
    /// <item><description>An array versus a non-array (after rules 1-2): never equal.</description></item>
    /// </list>
    /// <para>
    /// Also use this for the two constructs that compare loosely without an operator in
    /// sight: PHP's <c>switch</c>, and <c>in_array()</c> without its third argument (see
    /// <see cref="InArray"/>).
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// PRECISION LIMIT: numeric strings that do not fit in a 64-bit integer are compared as
    /// doubles, so two 19-digit strings differing in the last digit compare equal here.
    /// PHP has the same limit for floats but keeps integer precision further. Rare, and
    /// relevant if the source compares large ids as strings.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    public static bool LooseEquals(object? a, object? b)
    {
        return LooseEqualsCore(a, b, 0);
    }

    /// <summary>PHP's <c>!=</c> / <c>&lt;&gt;</c>. The negation of <see cref="LooseEquals"/>.</summary>
    public static bool LooseNotEquals(object? a, object? b)
    {
        return !LooseEqualsCore(a, b, 0);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// PHP's <c>===</c> — identical type and value.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The playbook's advice to map <c>===</c> onto C#'s <c>==</c> is right for the common
    /// cases (C# string <c>==</c> is value equality, like PHP's). It is wrong in two places,
    /// which is what this method is for:
    /// <list type="bullet">
    /// <item><description><c>1 === 1.0</c> is <c>false</c> in PHP (different types) and <c>1 == 1.0</c> is <c>true</c> in C#.</description></item>
    /// <item><description>Comparing two <c>object</c>-typed locals with <c>==</c> in C# is reference equality, so two equal boxed ints compare unequal — the opposite mistake.</description></item>
    /// </list>
    /// For two PHP objects, <c>===</c> means the same instance, which is reference equality
    /// and is what this returns. For arrays it means the same key/value pairs in the same
    /// order with the same types.
    /// </remarks>
    public static bool StrictEquals(object? a, object? b)
    {
        return StrictEqualsCore(a, b, 0);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// PHP's <c>&lt;=&gt;</c>, and therefore <c>&lt;</c>, <c>&gt;</c>, <c>&lt;=</c> and
    /// <c>&gt;=</c>, which coerce by the same table as <c>==</c>. Returns -1, 0 or 1.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Use this for a converted <c>usort()</c> comparator, and anywhere the source compared
    /// values of different types with a relational operator.
    /// <para>
    /// TWO THINGS IT CANNOT REPRODUCE. First, PHP compares strings with <c>strcmp</c>, which
    /// orders by UTF-8 bytes; this orders by UTF-16 code units. The two agree for ASCII and
    /// disagree for a handful of non-BMP cases. Second, PHP calls two arrays "uncomparable"
    /// when one has a key the other lacks, and the value it returns for that is not
    /// meaningfully defined; this returns 1. Do not build sorting logic on that case.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    public static int LooseCompare(object? a, object? b)
    {
        return LooseCompareCore(a, b, 0);
    }

    // ── String and number juggling ─────────────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// PHP's numeric coercion — what <c>(float)$x</c> and arithmetic on a string produce.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// A string contributes its leading numeric prefix and nothing else: <c>"12abc"</c> is
    /// <c>12</c>, <c>"abc"</c> is <c>0</c>, <c>"1.5e3x"</c> is <c>1500</c>. <c>null</c> is
    /// <c>0</c>; <c>true</c> is <c>1</c> and <c>false</c> is <c>0</c>.
    /// <para>
    /// NOT FAITHFUL FOR ARRAYS AND OBJECTS: PHP 8 raises a <c>TypeError</c> for
    /// <c>1 + []</c> — a fatal, not a coercion. This returns <c>0</c> for an array and
    /// <c>1</c> for any other object rather than throwing, because turning a legacy fatal
    /// into a .NET exception at a different point in the request is its own behaviour
    /// change. If the source really did arithmetic on an array, that line needs a human.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    public static double ToNumber(object? value)
    {
        if (value is null) return 0d;
        if (value is bool flag) return flag ? 1d : 0d;
        if (value is string text) return LeadingNumber(text);
        if (value is char character) return LeadingNumber(character.ToString());
        if (IsNumber(value))
        {
            try { return Convert.ToDouble(value, CultureInfo.InvariantCulture); }
            catch (OverflowException) { return 0d; }
            catch (InvalidCastException) { return 0d; }
        }
        if (IsPhpArray(value)) return 0d;
        return 1d;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// PHP's <c>intval()</c> / <c>(int)</c> cast.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Truncates toward zero rather than rounding: <c>(int)"12.9"</c> is <c>12</c> and
    /// <c>(int)-12.9</c> is <c>-12</c>. Reads a leading numeric prefix from a string, so
    /// <c>(int)"12abc"</c> is <c>12</c> and <c>(int)"abc"</c> is <c>0</c>; exponents count,
    /// so <c>(int)"1e3"</c> is <c>1000</c>.
    /// <para>
    /// PHP saturates at <c>PHP_INT_MAX</c> / <c>PHP_INT_MIN</c> rather than wrapping, and so
    /// does this. <c>(int)NAN</c> and <c>(int)INF</c> are <c>0</c> in PHP 8. An array casts
    /// to <c>0</c> when empty and <c>1</c> otherwise, which is a genuine PHP oddity rather
    /// than an approximation.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    public static long IntVal(object? value)
    {
        if (value is null) return 0L;
        if (value is bool flag) return flag ? 1L : 0L;
        if (IsIntegral(value))
        {
            try { return Convert.ToInt64(value, CultureInfo.InvariantCulture); }
            catch (OverflowException) { return long.MaxValue; }
        }
        if (IsPhpArray(value)) return Count(value) == 0 ? 0L : 1L;
        double number = ToNumber(value);
        if (double.IsNaN(number) || double.IsInfinity(number)) return 0L;
        if (number >= 9.2233720368547758e18) return long.MaxValue;
        if (number <= -9.2233720368547758e18) return long.MinValue;
        return (long)Math.Truncate(number);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// PHP's string coercion — what <c>echo</c>, <c>(string)</c> and the <c>.</c> operator do.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The three that catch people out: <c>true</c> becomes <c>"1"</c>, <c>false</c> becomes
    /// the EMPTY STRING (not <c>"False"</c>, which is what C#'s <c>ToString()</c> gives), and
    /// <c>null</c> becomes the empty string. So <c>"x" . $flag</c> is <c>"x1"</c> or
    /// <c>"x"</c>, never <c>"xFalse"</c>.
    /// <para>
    /// FLOATS ARE NOT ROUND-TRIPPED. PHP prints a float with <c>precision</c> significant
    /// digits (default 14), which is why <c>echo 0.1 + 0.2;</c> prints <c>0.3</c> in PHP and
    /// <c>0.30000000000000004</c> from C#'s default <c>ToString()</c>. This reproduces PHP,
    /// which means it is lossy on purpose — do not use it to serialise a value you intend to
    /// parse back. <c>INF</c>, <c>-INF</c> and <c>NAN</c> print as PHP prints them.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// An array becomes the literal string <c>"Array"</c>, as in PHP (which also emits a
    /// notice this cannot reproduce). C#'s <c>decimal</c> has no PHP equivalent at all and is
    /// formatted with its own full precision.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    public static string ToPhpString(object? value)
    {
        if (value is null) return "";
        if (value is bool flag) return flag ? "1" : "";
        if (value is string text) return text;
        if (value is char character) return character.ToString();
        if (value is float single) return DoubleToPhpString(single);
        if (value is double number) return DoubleToPhpString(number);
        if (value is decimal fixedPoint) return fixedPoint.ToString("G", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);
        if (IsIntegral(value)) return Convert.ToString(value, CultureInfo.InvariantCulture) ?? "";
        if (IsPhpArray(value)) return "Array";
        return value.ToString() ?? "";
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// PHP's <c>.</c> concatenation operator, which coerces every operand with
    /// <see cref="ToPhpString"/> first.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// C#'s <c>+</c> on <c>object</c> calls <c>ToString()</c>, so a <c>bool</c> concatenated
    /// in C# produces <c>"True"</c> where PHP produced <c>"1"</c>, and a <c>null</c> produces
    /// <c>""</c> in both — one of the two is right by accident.
    /// </remarks>
    public static string Concat(params object?[] parts)
    {
        if (parts is null) return "";
        var builder = new StringBuilder();
        foreach (object? part in parts) builder.Append(ToPhpString(part));
        return builder.ToString();
    }

    // ── The false-not-null sentinel ────────────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// PHP's <c>strpos()</c>. Returns the offset, or <c>null</c> when the needle is absent.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// PHP signals "not found" with <c>false</c> — not <c>null</c> and not <c>-1</c> — and
    /// that single fact produces two different silent bugs on the way to C#:
    /// <list type="bullet">
    /// <item><description>
    /// <c>if (strpos($h, $n) === false)</c> is the correct PHP idiom. Ported naively to
    /// <c>if (h.IndexOf(n) == 0)</c> it means the opposite; ported to
    /// <c>if (h.IndexOf(n) &lt; 1)</c> it swallows a match at offset 0. Returning
    /// <c>int?</c> makes "not found" unrepresentable as a number, so the check has to be
    /// <c>HasValue</c> and cannot be got backwards.
    /// </description></item>
    /// <item><description>
    /// <c>if (strpos($h, $n))</c> — no <c>=== false</c> — is a BUG IN THE SOURCE: it is
    /// false when the match is at offset 0, because <c>0</c> is falsy. A faithful port
    /// reproduces the bug. Decide deliberately which you want, and say so.
    /// </description></item>
    /// </list>
    /// <para>
    /// OFFSETS ARE CHARACTERS HERE, BYTES IN PHP. PHP's <c>strpos</c> returns a byte offset,
    /// so on non-ASCII text it disagrees with this. That is deliberate and self-consistent:
    /// <see cref="SubstrPhp"/> also indexes by character, so a converted
    /// <c>substr($s, strpos($s, $x))</c> pair still lines up. It matches <c>mb_strpos</c>
    /// rather than <c>strpos</c>. If the source did byte arithmetic on the result — mixing it
    /// with <c>strlen</c>, for instance — that arithmetic needs a human.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Where the intent is only presence, use <c>string.Contains</c> and say so in the
    /// conversion notes; it is clearer and cannot be got backwards at all.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// PHP 8 throws a <c>ValueError</c> for an offset outside the string; this clamps a
    /// negative one to 0 and returns <c>null</c> for one past the end. An empty needle
    /// returns the offset, as PHP 8 does (PHP 7 returned <c>false</c>).
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    public static int? StrPos(string? haystack, string? needle, int offset = 0)
    {
        string subject = haystack ?? "";
        string search = needle ?? "";
        int start = offset;
        if (start < 0)
        {
            start = subject.Length + start;
            if (start < 0) start = 0;
        }
        if (start > subject.Length) return null;
        if (search.Length == 0) return start;
        int index = subject.IndexOf(search, start, StringComparison.Ordinal);
        return index < 0 ? (int?)null : index;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// PHP's <c>array_search()</c>. Returns the key, or <c>null</c> when the value is absent.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Same sentinel trap as <see cref="StrPos"/>, and worse in practice: PHP returns
    /// <c>false</c> on failure and a key of <c>0</c> on a hit at the first position, so
    /// <c>if (array_search(...))</c> misses the first element. The key is boxed — a
    /// <c>long</c> index for a list, the dictionary key for a map.
    /// <para>
    /// The default comparison is LOOSE, as in PHP. Pass <c>strict: true</c> for
    /// <c>array_search($n, $h, true)</c>.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    public static object? ArraySearch(object? needle, object? haystack, bool strict = false)
    {
        foreach (KeyValuePair<object, object?> entry in Entries(haystack))
        {
            bool hit = strict ? StrictEqualsCore(needle, entry.Value, 0)
                              : LooseEqualsCore(needle, entry.Value, 0);
            if (hit) return entry.Key;
        }
        return null;
    }

    // ── Arrays: one PHP type doing three C# jobs ───────────────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// PHP's <c>count()</c>.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Handles the list case and the dictionary case, because a PHP array is both.
    /// <para>
    /// NOT FAITHFUL AT THE EDGES, deliberately: <c>count(null)</c> and <c>count("abc")</c>
    /// both raise a <c>TypeError</c> in PHP 8 (they returned <c>0</c> and <c>1</c> in PHP 7).
    /// This returns <c>0</c> and <c>1</c> — the PHP 7 answers — because a converted
    /// application that throws where the legacy one returned a number is a new outage, and
    /// the run's own validation is a better place to surface the ambiguity than production.
    /// If the source targeted PHP 8, a <c>count()</c> that could see <c>null</c> was already
    /// a latent fatal and is worth a look.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    public static int Count(object? value)
    {
        if (value is null) return 0;
        if (value is IDictionary dictionary) return dictionary.Count;
        if (value is string) return 1;
        if (value is ICollection collection) return collection.Count;
        if (value is IEnumerable sequence)
        {
            int total = 0;
            foreach (object? unused in sequence) total++;
            return total;
        }
        return 1;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// PHP's <c>in_array()</c>. Loose by default — which is the whole trap.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <c>in_array()</c> without a third argument compares with <c>==</c>, so under PHP 7
    /// <c>in_array("abc", [0])</c> was <c>true</c>. Under PHP 8 (what this implements) it is
    /// <c>false</c>. If the source relied on the loose match to find a numeric string in a
    /// list of ints — <c>in_array($_GET['id'], $allowedIds)</c> is the classic — it still
    /// works, because a numeric string still compares numerically. If it relied on the PHP 7
    /// string-to-zero behaviour in a permission check, it does not, and it should not.
    /// <para>Pass <c>strict: true</c> for <c>in_array($n, $h, true)</c>.</para>
    /// </remarks>
    public static bool InArray(object? needle, object? haystack, bool strict = false)
    {
        foreach (KeyValuePair<object, object?> entry in Entries(haystack))
        {
            bool hit = strict ? StrictEqualsCore(needle, entry.Value, 0)
                              : LooseEqualsCore(needle, entry.Value, 0);
            if (hit) return true;
        }
        return false;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// PHP's <c>array_key_exists()</c> — the key is present, even if its value is
    /// <c>null</c>.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Not the same question as <see cref="Isset"/>, and confusing the two is a real source
    /// of behaviour change. Keys are matched loosely, because PHP normalises <c>"0"</c> and
    /// <c>0</c> to the same key.
    /// </remarks>
    public static bool ArrayKeyExists(object? key, object? array)
    {
        foreach (KeyValuePair<object, object?> entry in Entries(array))
        {
            if (LooseEqualsCore(key, entry.Key, 0)) return true;
        }
        return false;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// PHP's <c>isset()</c> on an array element — the key is present AND its value is not
    /// <c>null</c>.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <c>isset($a['k'])</c> is <c>false</c> when <c>$a['k']</c> is <c>null</c>, while
    /// <c>array_key_exists('k', $a)</c> is <c>true</c>. A converted
    /// <c>dictionary.ContainsKey("k")</c> reproduces the second, not the first — which
    /// matters wherever a nullable database column was stored into the array.
    /// </remarks>
    public static bool Isset(object? array, object? key)
    {
        foreach (KeyValuePair<object, object?> entry in Entries(array))
        {
            if (LooseEqualsCore(key, entry.Key, 0)) return entry.Value is not null;
        }
        return false;
    }

    // ── Strings: length is three different things ──────────────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// PHP's <c>strlen()</c> — the number of BYTES, not characters.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <c>strlen("café")</c> is <c>5</c> on a UTF-8 codebase, while <c>mb_strlen</c> gives
    /// <c>4</c> and C#'s <c>.Length</c> gives <c>4</c>. So a validation rule written as
    /// <c>strlen($name) &lt;= 50</c> admits fewer accented characters than a port to
    /// <c>name.Length &lt;= 50</c> does, and a column sized from it can overflow.
    /// <para>
    /// ASSUMES UTF-8, because that is what a .NET string encodes to and what the export
    /// targets. A legacy PHP application on latin1 — extremely common with older MySQL — had
    /// a different byte count for exactly the same text, so if the source's encoding was not
    /// UTF-8, this number is right for the new system and different from the old one.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    public static int StrLen(string? value)
    {
        return value is null ? 0 : Encoding.UTF8.GetByteCount(value);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// PHP's <c>mb_strlen()</c> — the number of Unicode code points.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Differs from C#'s <c>.Length</c>, which counts UTF-16 code units: an emoji or any
    /// character above U+FFFF counts <c>1</c> here and <c>2</c> from <c>.Length</c>.
    /// <para>
    /// Also NOT the number of user-visible characters. A grapheme cluster — an emoji with a
    /// skin-tone modifier, or a base letter plus a combining accent — is several code points
    /// and counts as several here, which is what <c>mb_strlen</c> does. If you want what a
    /// human would count, that is <c>StringInfo.LengthInTextElements</c>, and it is a
    /// different number from what the legacy system produced.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    public static int MbStrLen(string? value)
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(value)) return 0;
        int total = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < value.Length; i++)
        {
            if (char.IsHighSurrogate(value[i]) && i + 1 < value.Length && char.IsLowSurrogate(value[i + 1])) i++;
            total++;
        }
        return total;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// PHP's <c>substr()</c>, including the negative offsets and negative lengths that make
    /// <c>string.Substring</c> throw.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <c>substr($s, -3)</c> is the last three characters; <c>substr($s, 1, -1)</c> drops the
    /// first and last. Both throw <c>ArgumentOutOfRangeException</c> from
    /// <c>string.Substring</c>, so a converted file that "looks fine" fails at runtime on the
    /// first row that exercises the path. An out-of-range start returns the empty string, as
    /// PHP 8 does (PHP 5 returned <c>false</c> here, which was another sentinel).
    /// <para>
    /// OFFSETS ARE CHARACTERS HERE, BYTES IN PHP. <c>substr()</c> slices bytes, so on UTF-8
    /// text it can cut a multi-byte character in half and produce an invalid string — which
    /// is a bug the legacy system had and this does not reproduce. This behaves like
    /// <c>mb_substr</c>, and is consistent with <see cref="StrPos"/>. If the source's offsets
    /// came from <see cref="StrLen"/> or a byte-oriented protocol, they do not mean what they
    /// meant, and that line needs a human.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>The C# range idiom <c>s[^3..]</c> covers the simple negative-offset case and is
    /// clearer where the offsets are literals.</para>
    /// </remarks>
    public static string SubstrPhp(string? value, int start, int? length = null)
    {
        string subject = value ?? "";
        int total = subject.Length;
        int begin = start;
        if (begin < 0)
        {
            begin = total + begin;
            if (begin < 0) begin = 0;
        }
        if (begin >= total) return "";

        int take;
        if (length is null)
        {
            take = total - begin;
        }
        else if (length.Value < 0)
        {
            take = total + length.Value - begin;
        }
        else
        {
            take = Math.Min(length.Value, total - begin);
        }
        if (take <= 0) return "";
        return subject.Substring(begin, take);
    }

    // ── Dates and time ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>PHP's <c>time()</c> — Unix seconds. Always UTC-based, timezone-independent.</summary>
    public static long Time()
    {
        return DateTimeOffset.UtcNow.ToUnixTimeSeconds();
    }

    /// <summary>"Now", in <see cref="ScriptTimeZone"/>. Read that property's warning first.</summary>
    public static DateTimeOffset Now()
    {
        return TimeZoneInfo.ConvertTime(DateTimeOffset.UtcNow, ScriptTimeZone);
    }

    /// <summary>A Unix timestamp as a moment in <see cref="ScriptTimeZone"/>.</summary>
    public static DateTimeOffset FromUnix(long unixSeconds)
    {
        return TimeZoneInfo.ConvertTime(DateTimeOffset.FromUnixTimeSeconds(unixSeconds), ScriptTimeZone);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// PHP's <c>date()</c>. Formats with PHP's format characters, not .NET's.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// PHP's format letters are not .NET custom format specifiers and overlap misleadingly:
    /// <c>d</c> means the day in both, but <c>m</c> is the month in PHP and the minute in
    /// .NET, <c>i</c> is the minute in PHP and nothing in .NET, and <c>Y</c> is a four-digit
    /// year in PHP and an era in .NET. Passing a PHP format string to
    /// <c>DateTime.ToString</c> therefore produces plausible garbage rather than an error.
    /// <para>
    /// Supported: <c>d j D l N S w z W F m M n t L o Y y a A g G h H i s u v e I O P T Z c r U</c>,
    /// and <c>\</c> escapes the next character. Anything else is emitted literally, which is
    /// also what PHP does.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// TIMEZONE. The moment is converted into <see cref="ScriptTimeZone"/> first, reproducing
    /// PHP's script-global timezone. A <c>DATETIME</c> read from MySQL carries no offset and
    /// arrives as <c>DateTimeKind.Unspecified</c>, so it is the caller's job to attach the
    /// right offset before calling this — every <c>date()</c> applied to a database value in
    /// the source depended on the legacy server's timezone setting, and that dependency does
    /// not travel with the data.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// <c>T</c> (timezone abbreviation, "EST") HAS NO .NET EQUIVALENT — <c>TimeZoneInfo</c>
    /// exposes full names, not abbreviations, and they differ by platform. It is rendered as
    /// the numeric offset instead, so output containing <c>T</c> will not match the legacy
    /// system character for character.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Names are always English, matching PHP's <c>date()</c> (which, unlike <c>strftime</c>,
    /// is not localised). This is a deliberate use of the invariant culture: a converted app
    /// running under a Turkish or German locale must still print "Monday".
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    public static string FormatDate(string? format, DateTimeOffset? when = null)
    {
        DateTimeOffset moment = when.HasValue
            ? TimeZoneInfo.ConvertTime(when.Value, ScriptTimeZone)
            : Now();
        CultureInfo invariant = CultureInfo.InvariantCulture;
        string pattern = format ?? "";
        var output = new StringBuilder(pattern.Length + 16);

        for (int i = 0; i < pattern.Length; i++)
        {
            char token = pattern[i];
            if (token == '\\')
            {
                i++;
                if (i < pattern.Length) output.Append(pattern[i]);
                continue;
            }
            switch (token)
            {
                case 'd': output.Append(moment.Day.ToString("00", invariant)); break;
                case 'j': output.Append(moment.Day.ToString(invariant)); break;
                case 'D': output.Append(invariant.DateTimeFormat.AbbreviatedDayNames[(int)moment.DayOfWeek]); break;
                case 'l': output.Append(invariant.DateTimeFormat.DayNames[(int)moment.DayOfWeek]); break;
                case 'N': output.Append((moment.DayOfWeek == DayOfWeek.Sunday ? 7 : (int)moment.DayOfWeek).ToString(invariant)); break;
                case 'S': output.Append(OrdinalSuffix(moment.Day)); break;
                case 'w': output.Append(((int)moment.DayOfWeek).ToString(invariant)); break;
                case 'z': output.Append((moment.DayOfYear - 1).ToString(invariant)); break;
                case 'W': output.Append(ISOWeek.GetWeekOfYear(moment.DateTime).ToString("00", invariant)); break;
                case 'F': output.Append(invariant.DateTimeFormat.MonthNames[moment.Month - 1]); break;
                case 'm': output.Append(moment.Month.ToString("00", invariant)); break;
                case 'M': output.Append(invariant.DateTimeFormat.AbbreviatedMonthNames[moment.Month - 1]); break;
                case 'n': output.Append(moment.Month.ToString(invariant)); break;
                case 't': output.Append(DateTime.DaysInMonth(moment.Year, moment.Month).ToString(invariant)); break;
                case 'L': output.Append(DateTime.IsLeapYear(moment.Year) ? "1" : "0"); break;
                case 'o': output.Append(ISOWeek.GetYear(moment.DateTime).ToString(invariant)); break;
                case 'Y': output.Append(moment.Year.ToString(invariant)); break;
                case 'y': output.Append((moment.Year % 100).ToString("00", invariant)); break;
                case 'a': output.Append(moment.Hour < 12 ? "am" : "pm"); break;
                case 'A': output.Append(moment.Hour < 12 ? "AM" : "PM"); break;
                case 'g': output.Append(Hour12(moment.Hour).ToString(invariant)); break;
                case 'G': output.Append(moment.Hour.ToString(invariant)); break;
                case 'h': output.Append(Hour12(moment.Hour).ToString("00", invariant)); break;
                case 'H': output.Append(moment.Hour.ToString("00", invariant)); break;
                case 'i': output.Append(moment.Minute.ToString("00", invariant)); break;
                case 's': output.Append(moment.Second.ToString("00", invariant)); break;
                case 'v': output.Append(moment.Millisecond.ToString("000", invariant)); break;
                case 'u': output.Append(((moment.Ticks % TimeSpan.TicksPerSecond) / 10).ToString("000000", invariant)); break;
                case 'e': output.Append(ScriptTimeZone.Id); break;
                case 'I': output.Append(ScriptTimeZone.IsDaylightSavingTime(moment) ? "1" : "0"); break;
                case 'O': output.Append(FormatOffset(moment.Offset, false)); break;
                case 'P': output.Append(FormatOffset(moment.Offset, true)); break;
                case 'T': output.Append(FormatOffset(moment.Offset, false)); break;
                case 'Z': output.Append(((int)moment.Offset.TotalSeconds).ToString(invariant)); break;
                case 'c': output.Append(FormatDate("Y-m-d\\TH:i:sP", moment)); break;
                case 'r': output.Append(FormatDate("D, d M Y H:i:s O", moment)); break;
                case 'U': output.Append(moment.ToUnixTimeSeconds().ToString(invariant)); break;
                default: output.Append(token); break;
            }
        }
        return output.ToString();
    }

    /// <summary>PHP's <c>date()</c> applied to a Unix timestamp.</summary>
    public static string FormatDate(string? format, long unixSeconds)
    {
        return FormatDate(format, FromUnix(unixSeconds));
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// A deliberately small subset of PHP's <c>strtotime()</c>. Returns <c>null</c> where PHP
    /// would return <c>false</c>, and also where this cannot parse something PHP could.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// PHP's parser is enormous and accepts far more than <c>DateTime.Parse</c>:
    /// "next thursday", "first day of next month", "+3 weeks 2 days", "last day of february
    /// 2021". Reimplementing it is not a conversion task, and a partial implementation that
    /// SILENTLY returns a plausible-but-wrong date is worse than one that refuses.
    /// <para>Handled here, and nothing else:</para>
    /// <list type="bullet">
    /// <item><description><c>now</c>, <c>today</c>, <c>midnight</c>, <c>tomorrow</c>, <c>yesterday</c>.</description></item>
    /// <item><description><c>@1700000000</c> — an explicit Unix timestamp.</description></item>
    /// <item><description>A single relative term: <c>+3 days</c>, <c>-1 week</c>, <c>2 hours ago</c>.</description></item>
    /// <item><description>ISO-ish absolute forms: <c>2026-01-02</c>, <c>2026-01-02 03:04</c>, <c>2026-01-02 03:04:05</c>, <c>2026-01-02T03:04:05</c>, and the same with an explicit <c>Z</c> or <c>+hh:mm</c> offset.</description></item>
    /// <item><description><c>01/02/2026</c>, read as <c>m/d/Y</c>, and <c>01-02-2026</c>, read as <c>d-m-Y</c> — PHP's rule is that a slash means American order and a dash means European. THIS IS THE AMBIGUITY MOST LIKELY TO BE WRONG in a converted app: the same eight characters mean two different days, and if the legacy data came from a form with no format hint, no code can settle it.</description></item>
    /// </list>
    /// <para>
    /// Anything else returns <c>null</c>. Treat every call site as needing a look — the
    /// playbook already says to mark converted <c>strtotime</c> calls for manual follow-up,
    /// and this method is not a reason to skip that.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>Absolute forms without an offset are interpreted in <see cref="ScriptTimeZone"/>,
    /// as PHP does.</para>
    /// </remarks>
    public static DateTimeOffset? StrToTime(string? input, DateTimeOffset? relativeTo = null)
    {
        string text = (input ?? "").Trim();
        if (text.Length == 0) return null;

        DateTimeOffset baseline = relativeTo.HasValue
            ? TimeZoneInfo.ConvertTime(relativeTo.Value, ScriptTimeZone)
            : Now();
        string lowered = text.ToLowerInvariant();

        switch (lowered)
        {
            case "now": return baseline;
            case "today": return StartOfDay(baseline);
            case "midnight": return StartOfDay(baseline);
            case "tomorrow": return StartOfDay(baseline).AddDays(1);
            case "yesterday": return StartOfDay(baseline).AddDays(-1);
        }

        if (lowered.Length > 1 && lowered[0] == '@'
            && long.TryParse(lowered.Substring(1), NumberStyles.AllowLeadingSign,
                             CultureInfo.InvariantCulture, out long epoch))
        {
            return FromUnix(epoch);
        }

        Match relative = RelativeTimePattern.Match(lowered);
        if (relative.Success)
        {
            long amount = long.Parse(relative.Groups[1].Value, NumberStyles.AllowLeadingSign,
                                     CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);
            if (relative.Groups[3].Success) amount = -amount;
            switch (relative.Groups[2].Value)
            {
                case "second": case "sec": return baseline.AddSeconds(amount);
                case "minute": case "min": return baseline.AddMinutes(amount);
                case "hour": return baseline.AddHours(amount);
                case "day": return baseline.AddDays(amount);
                case "week": return baseline.AddDays(amount * 7);
                case "month": return baseline.AddMonths((int)amount);
                case "year": return baseline.AddYears((int)amount);
            }
        }

        // An explicit offset (or trailing Z) wins over the script timezone, as in PHP.
        if (HasExplicitOffset(text)
            && DateTimeOffset.TryParse(text, CultureInfo.InvariantCulture,
                                       DateTimeStyles.RoundtripKind, out DateTimeOffset absolute))
        {
            return TimeZoneInfo.ConvertTime(absolute, ScriptTimeZone);
        }

        string[] formats =
        {
            "yyyy-MM-dd", "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm", "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss",
            "yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm", "yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm:ss",
            "yyyy/MM/dd", "MM/dd/yyyy", "MM/dd/yyyy HH:mm", "MM/dd/yyyy HH:mm:ss",
            "dd-MM-yyyy", "dd-MM-yyyy HH:mm", "dd-MM-yyyy HH:mm:ss",
        };
        if (DateTime.TryParseExact(text, formats, CultureInfo.InvariantCulture,
                                   DateTimeStyles.None, out DateTime naive))
        {
            DateTime unspecified = DateTime.SpecifyKind(naive, DateTimeKind.Unspecified);
            return new DateTimeOffset(unspecified, ScriptTimeZone.GetUtcOffset(unspecified));
        }

        return null;
    }

    // ── Internals ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    private static bool LooseEqualsCore(object? a, object? b, int depth)
    {
        bool leftNull = a is null;
        bool rightNull = b is null;
        bool leftString = a is string || a is char;
        bool rightString = b is string || b is char;

        // PHP 8 keeps a dedicated null-versus-string row, applied BEFORE the boolean rule.
        // It is why `null == ""` is true and `null == "0"` is false.
        if (leftNull && rightString) return StringsLooseEqual("", StringValue(b));
        if (rightNull && leftString) return StringsLooseEqual(StringValue(a), "");

        if (leftNull || rightNull || a is bool || b is bool) return Truthy(a) == Truthy(b);

        bool leftNumber = IsNumber(a);
        bool rightNumber = IsNumber(b);

        if (leftNumber && rightNumber) return NumbersEqual(a!, b!);
        if (leftNumber && rightString) return NumberEqualsString(a!, StringValue(b));
        if (rightNumber && leftString) return NumberEqualsString(b!, StringValue(a));
        if (leftString && rightString) return StringsLooseEqual(StringValue(a), StringValue(b));

        bool leftArray = IsPhpArray(a);
        bool rightArray = IsPhpArray(b);
        if (leftArray && rightArray) return ArraysLooseEqual(a!, b!, depth);
        if (leftArray || rightArray) return false;

        return Equals(a, b);
    }

    private static bool StrictEqualsCore(object? a, object? b, int depth)
    {
        if (a is null || b is null) return a is null && b is null;
        if (a is bool || b is bool) return a is bool left && b is bool right && left == right;

        bool leftString = a is string || a is char;
        bool rightString = b is string || b is char;
        if (leftString || rightString)
        {
            return leftString && rightString
                && string.Equals(StringValue(a), StringValue(b), StringComparison.Ordinal);
        }

        bool leftNumber = IsNumber(a);
        bool rightNumber = IsNumber(b);
        if (leftNumber || rightNumber)
        {
            if (!(leftNumber && rightNumber)) return false;
            // PHP distinguishes int from float, so `1 === 1.0` is false.
            if (IsIntegral(a) != IsIntegral(b)) return false;
            return NumbersEqual(a, b);
        }

        bool leftArray = IsPhpArray(a);
        bool rightArray = IsPhpArray(b);
        if (leftArray || rightArray)
        {
            if (!(leftArray && rightArray)) return false;
            if (depth > MaxComparisonDepth) return false;
            List<KeyValuePair<object, object?>> first = Entries(a);
            List<KeyValuePair<object, object?>> second = Entries(b);
            if (first.Count != second.Count) return false;
            for (int i = 0; i < first.Count; i++)
            {
                if (!StrictEqualsCore(first[i].Key, second[i].Key, depth + 1)) return false;
                if (!StrictEqualsCore(first[i].Value, second[i].Value, depth + 1)) return false;
            }
            return true;
        }

        return ReferenceEquals(a, b);
    }

    private static int LooseCompareCore(object? a, object? b, int depth)
    {
        bool leftNull = a is null;
        bool rightNull = b is null;
        bool leftString = a is string || a is char;
        bool rightString = b is string || b is char;

        if (leftNull && rightString) return StringsCompare("", StringValue(b));
        if (rightNull && leftString) return StringsCompare(StringValue(a), "");

        if (leftNull || rightNull || a is bool || b is bool)
        {
            return Truthy(a).CompareTo(Truthy(b));
        }

        bool leftNumber = IsNumber(a);
        bool rightNumber = IsNumber(b);

        if (leftNumber && rightNumber) return NumbersCompare(a!, b!);
        if (leftNumber && rightString)
        {
            string other = StringValue(b);
            return IsNumericString(other)
                ? NumbersCompare(a!, ParseNumericString(other))
                : Sign(string.CompareOrdinal(ToPhpString(a), other));
        }
        if (rightNumber && leftString)
        {
            string other = StringValue(a);
            return IsNumericString(other)
                ? NumbersCompare(ParseNumericString(other), b!)
                : Sign(string.CompareOrdinal(other, ToPhpString(b)));
        }
        if (leftString && rightString) return StringsCompare(StringValue(a), StringValue(b));

        bool leftArray = IsPhpArray(a);
        bool rightArray = IsPhpArray(b);
        if (leftArray && rightArray)
        {
            if (depth > MaxComparisonDepth) return 0;
            List<KeyValuePair<object, object?>> first = Entries(a);
            List<KeyValuePair<object, object?>> second = Entries(b);
            if (first.Count != second.Count) return first.Count < second.Count ? -1 : 1;
            foreach (KeyValuePair<object, object?> entry in first)
            {
                bool found = false;
                foreach (KeyValuePair<object, object?> candidate in second)
                {
                    if (!LooseEqualsCore(entry.Key, candidate.Key, depth + 1)) continue;
                    found = true;
                    int order = LooseCompareCore(entry.Value, candidate.Value, depth + 1);
                    if (order != 0) return order;
                    break;
                }
                // PHP calls this "uncomparable" and its result is not usefully defined.
                if (!found) return 1;
            }
            return 0;
        }
        if (leftArray) return 1;
        if (rightArray) return -1;

        return 0;
    }

    private static bool NumbersEqual(object a, object b)
    {
        if (TryGetInt64(a, out long left) && TryGetInt64(b, out long right)) return left == right;
        return ToNumber(a) == ToNumber(b);
    }

    private static int NumbersCompare(object a, object b)
    {
        if (TryGetInt64(a, out long left) && TryGetInt64(b, out long right)) return left.CompareTo(right);
        return ToNumber(a).CompareTo(ToNumber(b));
    }

    private static bool NumberEqualsString(object number, string text)
    {
        // PHP 8: a numeric string compares numerically; anything else turns the NUMBER into
        // a string. That reversal is the whole `0 == "foo"` change.
        if (IsNumericString(text)) return NumbersEqual(number, ParseNumericString(text));
        return string.Equals(ToPhpString(number), text, StringComparison.Ordinal);
    }

    private static bool StringsLooseEqual(string a, string b)
    {
        if (IsNumericString(a) && IsNumericString(b))
        {
            return NumbersEqual(ParseNumericString(a), ParseNumericString(b));
        }
        return string.Equals(a, b, StringComparison.Ordinal);
    }

    private static int StringsCompare(string a, string b)
    {
        if (IsNumericString(a) && IsNumericString(b))
        {
            return NumbersCompare(ParseNumericString(a), ParseNumericString(b));
        }
        return Sign(string.CompareOrdinal(a, b));
    }

    private static bool ArraysLooseEqual(object a, object b, int depth)
    {
        if (depth > MaxComparisonDepth) return false;
        List<KeyValuePair<object, object?>> first = Entries(a);
        List<KeyValuePair<object, object?>> second = Entries(b);
        if (first.Count != second.Count) return false;

        // Quadratic, because PHP array keys are int-or-string and are matched loosely. Fine
        // for the sizes a converted page script deals in; not a bulk data structure.
        foreach (KeyValuePair<object, object?> entry in first)
        {
            bool matched = false;
            foreach (KeyValuePair<object, object?> candidate in second)
            {
                if (!LooseEqualsCore(entry.Key, candidate.Key, depth + 1)) continue;
                matched = LooseEqualsCore(entry.Value, candidate.Value, depth + 1);
                break;
            }
            if (!matched) return false;
        }
        return true;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether a value plays the part of a PHP array here: a dictionary, or any non-string
    /// sequence. AMBIGUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION — a converted domain type that happens to
    /// implement IEnumerable is treated as an array by comparison and truthiness, which PHP
    /// would not have done to an object. Keep converted entities out of loose comparisons.
    /// </summary>
    private static bool IsPhpArray(object? value)
    {
        return value is IDictionary || (value is IEnumerable && value is not string);
    }

    private static bool IsIntegral(object? value)
    {
        return value is sbyte or byte or short or ushort or int or uint or long or ulong;
    }

    private static bool IsNumber(object? value)
    {
        return IsIntegral(value) || value is float or double or decimal;
    }

    private static bool TryGetInt64(object? value, out long result)
    {
        result = 0L;
        try
        {
            if (value is ulong unsigned)
            {
                if (unsigned > long.MaxValue) return false;
                result = (long)unsigned;
                return true;
            }
            if (IsIntegral(value))
            {
                result = Convert.ToInt64(value, CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);
                return true;
            }
            if (value is float || value is double)
            {
                double number = Convert.ToDouble(value, CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);
                if (double.IsNaN(number) || double.IsInfinity(number)) return false;
                if (number != Math.Floor(number)) return false;
                if (number > 9.2233720368547748e18 || number < -9.2233720368547748e18) return false;
                result = (long)number;
                return true;
            }
            if (value is decimal fixedPoint)
            {
                if (fixedPoint != decimal.Truncate(fixedPoint)) return false;
                result = (long)fixedPoint;
                return true;
            }
        }
        catch (OverflowException)
        {
            return false;
        }
        catch (InvalidCastException)
        {
            return false;
        }
        return false;
    }

    private static string StringValue(object? value)
    {
        if (value is string text) return text;
        if (value is char character) return character.ToString();
        return ToPhpString(value);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// PHP 8's definition of a numeric string: optional surrounding whitespace, optional
    /// sign, digits with an optional decimal point, an optional exponent, nothing else.
    /// Hexadecimal is NOT numeric (it stopped being so in PHP 7), and neither are "INF" or
    /// "NAN". PHP 8.0 was the version that started allowing TRAILING whitespace.
    /// </summary>
    private static bool IsNumericString(string? value)
    {
        if (value is null) return false;
        string text = TrimPhpWhitespace(value);
        if (text.Length == 0) return false;

        int i = 0;
        if (text[i] == '+' || text[i] == '-') i++;
        int digits = 0;
        while (i < text.Length && text[i] >= '0' && text[i] <= '9') { i++; digits++; }
        if (i < text.Length && text[i] == '.')
        {
            i++;
            while (i < text.Length && text[i] >= '0' && text[i] <= '9') { i++; digits++; }
        }
        if (digits == 0) return false;
        if (i < text.Length && (text[i] == 'e' || text[i] == 'E'))
        {
            i++;
            if (i < text.Length && (text[i] == '+' || text[i] == '-')) i++;
            int exponentDigits = 0;
            while (i < text.Length && text[i] >= '0' && text[i] <= '9') { i++; exponentDigits++; }
            if (exponentDigits == 0) return false;
        }
        return i == text.Length;
    }

    private static object ParseNumericString(string value)
    {
        string text = TrimPhpWhitespace(value);
        if (long.TryParse(text, NumberStyles.AllowLeadingSign, CultureInfo.InvariantCulture,
                          out long integer))
        {
            return integer;
        }
        if (double.TryParse(text, NumberStyles.Float, CultureInfo.InvariantCulture,
                            out double number))
        {
            return number;
        }
        return 0L;
    }

    private static double LeadingNumber(string value)
    {
        string text = value.TrimStart(' ', '\t', '\n', '\r', '\v', '\f');
        int i = 0;
        if (i < text.Length && (text[i] == '+' || text[i] == '-')) i++;
        int digits = 0;
        while (i < text.Length && text[i] >= '0' && text[i] <= '9') { i++; digits++; }

        int end = i;
        if (i < text.Length && text[i] == '.')
        {
            int j = i + 1;
            int fraction = 0;
            while (j < text.Length && text[j] >= '0' && text[j] <= '9') { j++; fraction++; }
            if (digits + fraction > 0)
            {
                digits += fraction;
                i = j;
                end = j;
            }
        }
        if (digits == 0) return 0d;

        if (i < text.Length && (text[i] == 'e' || text[i] == 'E'))
        {
            int j = i + 1;
            if (j < text.Length && (text[j] == '+' || text[j] == '-')) j++;
            int exponentDigits = 0;
            while (j < text.Length && text[j] >= '0' && text[j] <= '9') { j++; exponentDigits++; }
            if (exponentDigits > 0) end = j;
        }

        string prefix = text.Substring(0, end);
        return double.TryParse(prefix, NumberStyles.Float, CultureInfo.InvariantCulture,
                               out double parsed) ? parsed : 0d;
    }

    private static string TrimPhpWhitespace(string value)
    {
        return value.Trim(' ', '\t', '\n', '\r', '\v', '\f');
    }

    private static string DoubleToPhpString(double value)
    {
        if (double.IsNaN(value)) return "NAN";
        if (double.IsPositiveInfinity(value)) return "INF";
        if (double.IsNegativeInfinity(value)) return "-INF";

        int precision = PhpPrecision;
        if (precision < 1) precision = 1;
        if (precision > 17) precision = 17;
        string text = value.ToString("G" + precision.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture),
                                     CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);

        int exponentAt = text.IndexOf('E');
        if (exponentAt < 0) return text;

        // .NET writes "1E+25"; PHP writes "1.0E+25" and does not zero-pad the exponent.
        string mantissa = text.Substring(0, exponentAt);
        string exponent = text.Substring(exponentAt + 1);
        if (mantissa.IndexOf('.') < 0) mantissa += ".0";
        char sign = '+';
        if (exponent.Length > 0 && (exponent[0] == '+' || exponent[0] == '-'))
        {
            sign = exponent[0];
            exponent = exponent.Substring(1);
        }
        exponent = exponent.TrimStart('0');
        if (exponent.Length == 0) exponent = "0";
        return mantissa + "E" + sign + exponent;
    }

    private static List<KeyValuePair<object, object?>> Entries(object? value)
    {
        var entries = new List<KeyValuePair<object, object?>>();
        if (value is IDictionary dictionary)
        {
            foreach (DictionaryEntry entry in dictionary)
            {
                entries.Add(new KeyValuePair<object, object?>(entry.Key, entry.Value));
            }
            return entries;
        }
        if (value is IEnumerable sequence && value is not string)
        {
            long index = 0;
            foreach (object? item in sequence)
            {
                entries.Add(new KeyValuePair<object, object?>(index, item));
                index++;
            }
            return entries;
        }
        return entries;
    }

    private static bool AnyElement(IEnumerable sequence)
    {
        IEnumerator enumerator = sequence.GetEnumerator();
        try
        {
            return enumerator.MoveNext();
        }
        finally
        {
            (enumerator as IDisposable)?.Dispose();
        }
    }

    private static bool HasExplicitOffset(string text)
    {
        if (text.EndsWith("Z", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)) return true;
        int timeAt = text.IndexOf('T');
        if (timeAt < 0) timeAt = text.IndexOf(':');
        if (timeAt < 0) return false;
        string tail = text.Substring(timeAt);
        return tail.IndexOf('+') >= 0 || tail.LastIndexOf('-') > 0;
    }

    private static DateTimeOffset StartOfDay(DateTimeOffset when)
    {
        var midnight = new DateTime(when.Year, when.Month, when.Day, 0, 0, 0, DateTimeKind.Unspecified);
        // A midnight that does not exist (the spring-forward gap) resolves to the standard
        // offset, which is what TimeZoneInfo does and is not obviously what PHP does.
        return new DateTimeOffset(midnight, ScriptTimeZone.GetUtcOffset(midnight));
    }

    private static string FormatOffset(TimeSpan offset, bool withColon)
    {
        char sign = offset < TimeSpan.Zero ? '-' : '+';
        TimeSpan magnitude = offset.Duration();
        string hours = ((int)magnitude.TotalHours).ToString("00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);
        string minutes = magnitude.Minutes.ToString("00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);
        return withColon ? sign + hours + ":" + minutes : sign + hours + minutes;
    }

    private static string OrdinalSuffix(int day)
    {
        if (day >= 11 && day <= 13) return "th";
        switch (day % 10)
        {
            case 1: return "st";
            case 2: return "nd";
            case 3: return "rd";
            default: return "th";
        }
    }

    private static int Hour12(int hour)
    {
        int value = hour % 12;
        return value == 0 ? 12 : value;
    }

    private static int Sign(int value)
    {
        if (value < 0) return -1;
        return value > 0 ? 1 : 0;
    }
}
"""


# The members published to the run's symbol registry, and what each one was in PHP.
#
# The `signature` is READ OUT OF THE C# ABOVE rather than restated here (see `_declaration`),
# so a registry row can never drift from the file it describes — the failure this whole
# module exists to prevent, in miniature.
#
# `source_symbol` earns its place: it makes the registry searchable by the PHP construct a
# reviewer is looking at, which is the direction the question actually gets asked in.
_MEMBERS: tuple = (
    ("PhpPrecision", "constant", "php.ini precision"),
    ("ScriptTimeZone", "constant", "date_default_timezone_set()"),
    ("Truthy", "method", "if ($x)"),
    ("Empty", "method", "empty()"),
    ("LooseEquals", "method", "=="),
    ("LooseNotEquals", "method", "!="),
    ("StrictEquals", "method", "==="),
    ("LooseCompare", "method", "<=>"),
    ("ToNumber", "method", "(float) cast"),
    ("IntVal", "method", "intval()"),
    ("ToPhpString", "method", "(string) cast"),
    ("Concat", "method", ". operator"),
    ("StrPos", "method", "strpos()"),
    ("ArraySearch", "method", "array_search()"),
    ("Count", "method", "count()"),
    ("InArray", "method", "in_array()"),
    ("ArrayKeyExists", "method", "array_key_exists()"),
    ("Isset", "method", "isset()"),
    ("StrLen", "method", "strlen()"),
    ("MbStrLen", "method", "mb_strlen()"),
    ("SubstrPhp", "method", "substr()"),
    ("Time", "method", "time()"),
    ("Now", "method", "date_create()"),
    ("FromUnix", "method", "date() on a timestamp"),
    ("FormatDate", "method", "date()"),
    ("StrToTime", "method", "strtotime()"),
)

_CS_LINES: tuple = tuple(_PHP_COMPAT_CS.splitlines())


def _member_name(declaration: str) -> str:
    """The member name out of a `public static ...` declaration line."""
    head = declaration.split("(", 1)[0]     # a method loses its parameter list
    head = head.split("{", 1)[0]            # a property loses its accessor block
    head = head.split("=", 1)[0]            # and its initialiser
    parts = head.replace("?", " ").replace("[", " ").replace("]", " ").split()
    return parts[-1] if parts else ""


def _declaration(member: str) -> str:
    """The C# declaration line for `member`, read out of the constant.

    Returns "" and warns when the member is registered but no longer declared — which would
    mean the symbol registry is advertising an API the file does not have, i.e. the original
    defect regrown inside the fix.
    """
    for line in _CS_LINES:
        stripped = line.strip()
        if not stripped.startswith("public static "):
            continue
        if _member_name(stripped) == member:
            return stripped
    logger.warning("%s is registered as a symbol but is not declared in %s",
                   member, PHP_COMPAT_FILENAME)
    return ""


def _safe_namespace_segment(raw: str) -> str:
    cleaned = "".join(ch if ch in _IDENTIFIER_CHARS else "_" for ch in raw.strip())
    cleaned = cleaned.strip("_")
    if not cleaned:
        return ""
    if cleaned[0].isdigit():
        cleaned = "_" + cleaned
    return cleaned


def _root_namespace(root_namespace: str) -> str:
    """A project name reduced to something a C# `namespace` declaration will accept.

    `scaffold/dotnet.py` writes the project name straight into `RootNamespace`, so anything
    that survives that survives this too; the sanitising is for the fallback case where the
    architect proposed a name with punctuation in it.
    """
    segments = [s for s in (_safe_namespace_segment(part)
                            for part in str(root_namespace or "").split(".")) if s]
    if not segments:
        logger.warning("Root namespace %r yielded no valid C# identifier; using %r",
                       root_namespace, _FALLBACK_ROOT_NAMESPACE)
        return _FALLBACK_ROOT_NAMESPACE
    return ".".join(segments)


def namespace_for(root_namespace: str) -> str:
    """The full namespace the helper is declared in, e.g. `Converted.Compat`."""
    return f"{_root_namespace(root_namespace)}.{PHP_COMPAT_NAMESPACE_SUFFIX}"


def _target_path(output_dir: str) -> str:
    directory = str(output_dir or "").replace("\\", "/").strip().strip("/")
    return f"{directory}/{PHP_COMPAT_FILENAME}" if directory else PHP_COMPAT_FILENAME


def emit(root_namespace: str, output_dir: str = "") -> Dict[str, Any]:
    """One scaffold artifact, shaped exactly like the ones `scaffold/dotnet.py` returns.

    `{"path": ..., "content": ..., "purpose": ...}` — the three keys `runner._scaffold_one`
    copies into `store.complete_unit`. Byte-identical for identical arguments: the content is
    a constant with one token substituted, so a re-run or a resume cannot produce a different
    file (`store.complete_unit` versions artifacts by path, and a scaffold that differed
    between passes would create a new version for no reason).

    `output_dir` is the directory the file lands in, relative to the export root, and it
    matters more than it looks: an SDK-style `.csproj` compiles `**/*.cs` UNDER ITS OWN
    DIRECTORY, so a path outside every project directory is a file that is never built —
    which is the same end state as not emitting it at all. Pass the entry project's
    directory, conventionally `f"{project}/{PHP_COMPAT_NAMESPACE_SUFFIX}"`.
    """
    namespace = namespace_for(root_namespace)
    path = _target_path(output_dir)
    if not output_dir:
        logger.warning(
            "%s is being emitted at the export root with no output_dir, which is outside "
            "every project directory — no .csproj will compile it. Pass the entry project's "
            "directory.", PHP_COMPAT_FILENAME,
        )
    return {
        "path": path,
        "content": _PHP_COMPAT_CS.replace(_NAMESPACE_TOKEN, namespace),
        "purpose": (
            "PHP runtime semantics (PHP 8 loose ==, truthiness, strpos/substr/strlen, "
            "date/strtotime) as one shared deterministic helper, so converted units call "
            "the same implementation instead of each re-deriving it."
        ),
    }


def symbols(root_namespace: str, output_dir: str = "") -> List[Dict[str, Any]]:
    """Symbol rows for `store.complete_unit`, so converted units can see the helper's API.

    Two things follow from registering these. `store.symbol_names` — which is what
    `validation.check_symbols` checks a unit's `references` against — starts containing
    `PhpCompat.LooseEquals`, so a unit that honestly declares it no longer reads as an
    unresolved symbol. And a reviewer reading the registry can see what the shim offers.

    Names are QUALIFIED (`PhpCompat.LooseEquals`) rather than bare. `check_symbols` accepts
    either — it falls back to the last dotted segment — but bare names would collide in
    `modernization_symbols` with any converted unit that exports its own `Count` or `Empty`,
    and `complete_unit` records that as a `symbol_conflict` warning. Qualified is also how
    the playbook tells the model to write the call.
    """
    path = _target_path(output_dir)
    # Declared explicitly rather than left to be inherited from the unit. These rows are
    # written by the *scaffold* unit, which has no `target_namespace` of its own — so without
    # this the run would hold thirty symbols it cannot compute an import for.
    namespace = namespace_for(root_namespace)
    rows: List[Dict[str, Any]] = [{
        "name": CLASS_NAME,
        "kind": "class",
        "signature": f"namespace {namespace}; public static class {CLASS_NAME}",
        "target_path": path,
        "source_symbol": "PHP runtime semantics",
        "namespace": namespace,
    }]
    for member, kind, source_symbol in _MEMBERS:
        declaration = _declaration(member)
        if not declaration:
            continue
        rows.append({
            "name": f"{CLASS_NAME}.{member}",
            # A settable static property is not really a `constant`, but the converter's
            # symbol vocabulary has no `property` and `method` would be worse. The signature
            # shows `{ get; set; }`, so nothing is hidden.
            "kind": kind,
            "signature": declaration,
            "target_path": path,
            "source_symbol": source_symbol,
            "namespace": namespace,
        })
    return rows


def is_applicable(source_language: str, charter: Dict[str, Any]) -> bool:
    """Whether this run should get `PhpCompat.cs` at all.

    Two conditions, both necessary:

    1. **The source is PHP.** A COBOL or Delphi run must not receive a PHP semantics shim —
       it would be dead code advertising rules that do not apply to the source it came from,
       and `symbols()` would put names in the registry that nothing should ever call.
    2. **The target is .NET.** This artifact is C#. The check reuses
       `playbooks.normalize_target` and `playbooks.DOTNET_ALIASES` rather than a private
       spelling list, which is D-61: the file ships exactly when the playbook that promises
       `PhpCompat` is the one in the system prompt. Two lists would eventually disagree, and
       the symptom — a run that gets the PHP playbook but no shim — is the defect this module
       was written to fix.

    A VB.NET target returns False. A `.cs` file in an SDK-style VB project is not a compile
    error; it is simply never compiled, which lands the run back at "the playbook promises an
    API that does not ship" with no error message to explain it. Emitting nothing and saying
    so is the honest outcome; a `PhpCompat.vb` would be a separate artifact.
    """
    language = (source_language or "").strip().lower()
    if language not in _PHP_SOURCE_LANGUAGES:
        return False

    charter = charter or {}
    target = " ".join(str(charter.get(key) or "")
                      for key in ("target_stack", "target_language"))
    normalized = playbooks.normalize_target(target)
    if not normalized:
        return False

    language_choice = playbooks.normalize_target(str(charter.get("target_language") or ""))
    if any(spelling in language_choice for spelling in _VB_TARGET_SPELLINGS):
        logger.info("Target language %r is VB.NET; %s is C# and would never be compiled.",
                    charter.get("target_language"), PHP_COMPAT_FILENAME)
        return False

    return any(alias in normalized for alias in playbooks.DOTNET_ALIASES)
